## What to look for

{{ axis_focus }}

## What counts as a finding

Review the change, not the file it lives in. A finding must be about a line
this branch changed, or about something the change should have done and did
not -- a missing test for new behaviour, an unhandled case the change
introduces. Those need no line number.

Problems in code the branch never touched are real and worth writing down,
but they belong under a `## Pre-existing` heading and are never Critical: the
run cannot fix what it did not write, so raising one there sends the build
round a repair loop it cannot finish, and then wakes a person. Put it in
`## Pre-existing`, say what you would do about it, and let someone decide
separately.

## How to report

Write markdown, starting with a `## Findings` heading. Your entire reply is the
review for this axis: no preamble, no tool narration, and nothing about the
other axes — each is being read by someone else, and repeating their work makes
the merged review unreadable.

Give each finding a severity of **Critical**, **Important** or
**Nice-to-have**, the file and line, the concrete problem, and the fix you would
make. When you flag a structural problem, name the restructuring rather than
only the symptom.

Reserve **Critical** for what must not ship on this axis: incorrect behaviour, a
security hole, data loss, a performance cliff in a hot path, or an incompatible
contract change with no migration. A Critical finding stops the run and asks a
human, so spending it on a preference wastes their attention.

Say plainly when there is nothing to report on this axis. "No findings" is a
useful review; inventing one to look thorough is not.

End with a single line, exactly one of:

```
VERDICT: clean
VERDICT: critical
```

`critical` means you reported at least one **Critical** finding on the changed
code. It is not a summary of how you feel about the change: a review whose
worst finding is Important is `clean`, and says so while keeping the finding.
The verdict is read back against your findings, and a line that disagrees with
them is overridden and logged.

